# tools/g/glow_shared_bound_witness.rish -- one bound, one value, however many modules publish it.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/g/glow_shared_bound_witness.rish
#
# The Glow front end publishes numeric bounds across its modules, and several of those names are
# declared in more than one module. `max_name_len` once stood fourteen times -- in `tokens.rye`,
# `rune_shape.rye`, `expr.rye` and eleven rune modules -- each spelling 64 on its own line, and one
# module was free to raise its copy alone. Three of the agreements lived in a `///` comment, which
# is a wish where this is a wall: raise one copy and the lexer accepts a name the shape parser
# refuses, while both files still read correct alone.
#
# That name has an owner now (`20260910.083221`): the lexer declares it, thirteen modules alias it,
# and it carries one value by construction, so it has left this census. Six names remain shared, and
# each is a pair of PEERS -- two rune modules bounding the same kind of thing -- where the name has
# no owner and an alias would invent an arbitrary dependency between equals. For those six, this
# wall is the mechanism.
#
# Every shared name agrees today, so it stands under a standing agreement rather than over a break.
# That is the point -- the first drift reds on the lap it arrives, where a comment would let it ship.

say "glow-shared-bound: Language EN -- the fence posts two modules share, compared rather than trusted."
say "glow-shared-bound: Style Gauge, Meter -- each leg names what it plants and what must refuse."
say "glow-shared-bound: Lens TAME -- bound everything, and say why the bound is that number."

say "glow-shared-bound: the control -- thirty-two behaviors on real git repositories in a pen..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/g/glow_shared_bound_control.sh"]
assert ctl.ok else "glow-shared-bound: the control refused --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"
assert ctl.out contains "legs_pass=32" else "glow-shared-bound: the control did not prove thirty-two behaviors --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"
assert ctl.out contains "legs_fail=0" else "glow-shared-bound: a control leg failed --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"

say "glow-shared-bound: and the living tree, read on metal..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/g/glow_shared_bound_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "glow-shared-bound: the scan would not run -- read its own lines\n${scan.out}\n${scan.err}"

# invariant: the gate is the scan's own verdict rather than a number copied here. An expected value
# pinned in a second place is a stale pin waiting for a tree that moved (REDS %341's class).
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "glow-shared-bound: two modules publish one bound at two values -- run with --names, then make them agree or rename one\n${scan.out}"

say "glow-shared-bound: and the census reads a real population, so a green means something..."
# invariant: a scan whose pattern stopped matching would report zero shared names and zero
# divergences, which is indistinguishable from a clean tree. The scan itself refuses a population of
# zero declarations now, proven from both sides in the control, so this leg asks only that some name
# is still shared -- structural, rather than naming one specimen. Naming a specimen is what this
# witness did until `20260910.083221`, and it went stale the same day `max_name_len` found an owner:
# an expected value pinned in a second place, which is the fault the line above warns about.
let named = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/g/glow_shared_bound_scan.sh" "--names"]
assert named.ok else "glow-shared-bound: the named reading would not run\n${named.out}\n${named.err}"
assert named.out contains "name agreed " else "glow-shared-bound: no Glow bound is published by two modules any more -- every shared name has found an owner, and this wall has nothing left to compare\n${named.out}"

say "GREEN: glow-shared-bound -- every bound two Glow modules publish carries one value."
